Introduction to operating systems
What is an Operating System?
•	An operating system acts as the interface between a computer's hardware and the user, ensuring the computer runs efficiently and is easy to use.
•	Early computers in the 1950s lacked operating systems, requiring manual resets and program loading for each task, which was highly inefficient.
Evolution and Functions of Operating Systems
•	Modern operating systems allow computers to run multiple applications simultaneously and interact with external devices like printers and keyboards.
•	They bridge the communication gap between humans and computers by translating user commands into binary language (0s and 1s) that computers understand.
Importance of OS Security
•	Operating system security is crucial for protecting files, controlling data access, and authenticating users to defend against threats like viruses and malware.
•	A strong understanding of OS is essential for security professionals to perform tasks such as configuring system security, managing firewalls, setting security policies, and conducting auditing to detect unusual behaviour.

Compare operating systems
Common Operating Systems
•	Windows and macOS: Both are widely used in personal and enterprise settings. Windows is closed-source, while macOS is partially open-source.
•	Linux: A completely open-source operating system, Linux is particularly important in the security industry due to its customizable distributions.
•	ChromeOS, Android, and iOS: ChromeOS is partially open-source and often used in education. Android and iOS are mobile operating systems, with Android being open-source and iOS partially open-source.
Operating Systems and Vulnerabilities
•	Inevitable Security Issues: All operating systems have security vulnerabilities, making regular updates crucial for protection.
•	Legacy Operating Systems: These are outdated systems that are no longer supported or updated, making them highly vulnerable to new threats. Organizations may still use them due to software compatibility issues.

The operating system at work
Booting the Computer
•	When you press the power button, the computer activates a special microchip (BIOS or UEFI) that contains booting instructions.
•	These instructions load a program called the bootloader, which then starts the operating system.
OS and Application Interaction
•	When you use an application, it sends your request to the operating system.
•	The OS interprets this request and directs it to the appropriate hardware component, which then sends information back to the OS and subsequently to the application.
Importance for Security Analysts
•	Understanding these processes is crucial for security analysts to identify vulnerabilities, such as those that can occur during the booting process.
•	It also helps in tracing security events by understanding the flow of information between applications, the OS, and hardware.

Requests to the operating system

Booting the Computer
•	When you turn on your computer, either a BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) or UEFI (Unified Extensible Firmware Interface) microchip is activated.
•	These chips contain loading instructions, such as verifying hardware health, and ultimately activate the bootloader, which then boots the operating system.
Completing a Task
•	The process of completing a task involves four parts: the user initiates a task, an application receives the request, the operating system interprets and directs the request to the hardware, and the hardware performs the processing.
•	After processing, the hardware sends the output back through the operating system to the application, which then displays the results to the user.
The OS at Work Behind the Scenes
•	The operating system works in the background, similar to how a car engine operates or a restaurant kitchen prepares food, without direct user observation.
•	It is crucial for interpreting user requests and ensuring tasks are completed, even though its processes are not always transparent to the user.

Resource allocation via the OS
OS as a Resource Manager
•	The OS balances and efficiently uses system resources, similar to how a person manages energy for different tasks.
•	It ensures that various programs, tasks, and processes share the CPU, memory, storage, and input/output bandwidth effectively.
OS Functions and User Visibility
•	The OS allocates and de-allocates resources for each program, a process largely hidden from the user.
•	Tools like the task manager allow users to see how resources are being utilized by different tasks, including memory and CPU usage.
Importance for Security Analysts
•	Understanding resource usage is crucial for security analysts to respond to incidents and troubleshoot applications.
•	For example, slow computer performance could indicate malware allocating system resources, which an analyst can identify by monitoring resource consumption.

Virtualization technology
Understanding Virtual Machines and Virtualization
•	A virtual machine is a software-based version of a physical computer, simulating hardware like CPU and storage.
•	Virtualization is the process of creating virtual representations of physical machines using software, allowing multiple VMs to run on a single physical computer by sharing its resources.
Benefits of Virtual Machines
•	Security: VMs provide isolated environments, or "sandboxes," which can contain malware and prevent it from affecting the host machine or other VMs.
•	Efficiency: VMs allow users to run multiple operating systems and applications simultaneously on one physical machine, streamlining tasks like testing and exploration.
Managing Virtual Machines
•	Hypervisors are software used to manage multiple virtual machines, allocate shared resources from the host machine, and connect virtual and physical hardware.
•	KVM (Kernel-based Virtual Machine) is an open-source hypervisor built into the Linux kernel, enabling VM creation on Linux operating systems without additional software.



The user interface
GUI versus CLI
User Interfaces: GUI vs. CLI
•	A user interface (UI) is a program that allows users to control the functions of an operating system.
•	Two main types are the Graphical User Interface (GUI) and the Command-Line Interface (CLI).
Graphical User Interface (GUI)
•	GUIs use icons and visual elements to manage tasks, similar to what you experience on personal computers and cell phones.
•	Components often include a start menu, taskbar, and desktop icons, allowing users to click and search for programs.
Command-Line Interface (CLI)
•	CLIs are text-based interfaces that use commands to interact with the computer, offering more flexibility and power than GUIs.
•	Unlike GUIs, CLIs allow for customization and the ability to complete multiple tasks simultaneously, which can be particularly useful for security analysts.

The command line in use
GUI vs. CLI: Display and Function
•	Display: GUIs feature icons and graphics for managing tasks, while CLIs are text-based and resemble lines of code.
•	Functionality: GUIs typically handle one request at a time, whereas CLIs can process multiple requests simultaneously, offering greater efficiency for complex tasks.
Advantages of CLI in Cybersecurity
•	Efficiency: CLIs can be more efficient for experienced users, especially when performing multiple tasks, such as creating numerous files, as they allow for multiple requests at once.
•	History File: The Linux CLI records a history file of all commands and actions, which is crucial for security analysts to review steps during incident response or to trace potential attacker activity.

