Introduction to Linux
 
This lesson introduces Linux, highlighting its significance in security and its core characteristics.
The Origins of Linux
•	Linux was developed in the early 1990s through the independent efforts of Linus Torvalds, who created the Linux kernel, and Richard Stallman, who initiated the GNU operating system.
•	Both aimed to create an open-source, accessible operating system, with their combined innovations forming what is now known as Linux.
Unique Aspects of Linux
•	Linux is an open-source operating system, allowing free access, use, sharing, and modification of its source code under the GNU Public License.
•	This open-source philosophy has fostered a large community of developers who collaborate to advance computing and create over 600 different distributions, or varieties, of Linux.
Linux in Security Roles
•	As a security analyst, you will use Linux to examine various logs for identifying system issues, such as error logs.
•	Linux is also crucial for verifying access and authorization within identity and access management systems, and for utilizing specialized distributions with tools for tasks like digital forensics or penetration testing.




Linux architecture
The User and Applications
•	User: As the user, you are the primary initiator of tasks and commands within the Linux operating system. Linux is designed as a multi-user system, allowing multiple individuals to utilize its resources concurrently.
•	Applications: These are programs designed to perform specific tasks, such as word processing or calculations. Applications in Linux are frequently distributed and managed through package managers.
Interacting with the System
•	Shell: The shell acts as a command-line interpreter, serving as your primary interface for communicating with the Linux system. It processes your commands and displays the results.
•	Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS): The FHS is responsible for organizing data within the Linux OS, much like a filing cabinet. It provides a structured way to store and access data efficiently.
Core System Management
•	Kernel: The kernel is a fundamental component of Linux that manages processes and memory. It communicates directly with the hardware to execute commands and uses drivers to enable applications to perform their functions.
•	Hardware: This refers to the physical components of your computer, such as the CPU, mouse, and keyboard, which the kernel interacts with to carry out operations.

Linux architecture explained
Linux Architecture Components
•	User: The individual interacting with the computer, initiating and managing tasks in a multi-user system.
•	Applications: Programs that perform specific tasks, often installed via a package manager in Linux.
System Interaction and Organization
•	Shell: The command-line interpreter that translates user commands into instructions for the kernel.
•	Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS): Organizes data by defining where files and directories are stored within the operating system.
Core System Management and Hardware
•	Kernel: Manages processes and memory, communicating with applications and controlling hardware functions.
•	Hardware: The physical components of a computer, categorized as peripheral (e.g., monitors) or internal (e.g., CPU, RAM, hard drive).

