Manage file content in Bash
Find what you need with Linux
The grep Command
•	The grep command searches a specified file for a particular string of characters and returns all lines containing that string.
•	For example, grep OS updates.txt would search the updates.txt file for lines containing "OS".
Piping in Linux
•	Piping sends the standard output of one command as the standard input to another command for further processing, represented by the vertical bar character (|).
•	This allows you to combine commands, such as ls | grep users, which would list files and directories and then filter that list to show only those containing the word "users".

Filter content in Linux
Filtering for Information
•	Filtering involves selecting data that matches specific conditions, such as file extensions or text strings.
•	This skill allows security analysts to quickly locate relevant information, like identifying all .txt files affected by a virus.
Key Linux Commands for Filtering
•	grep: This command searches a specified file for lines containing a particular string or text. For example, grep OS updates.txt will return all lines with "OS" in the updates.txt file.
•	Piping (|): The pipe command sends the standard output of one command as the standard input to another. When used with grep, it can help find files or directories with specific words in their names, such as ls /home/analyst/reports | grep users.
•	find: This command searches for directories and files based on various criteria, including specific strings in their names (-name for case-sensitive, -iname for case-insensitive) or modification time (-mtime for days, -mmin for minutes). For instance, find /home/analyst/projects -name "*log*" will locate all files containing "log" in their names within the projects directory.

