Authenticate and authorize users
File permissions and ownership


This video explains file and directory permissions in Linux, which are crucial for security analysts to protect sensitive information.
Understanding Permissions
•	Permissions and Authorization: Permissions define the type of access granted to files or directories, directly relating to authorization, which limits resource access in a system.
•	Types of Permissions: Linux has three types of permissions: read (view content), write (modify content or create new files), and execute (run files or enter directories).
Permission Owners and Representation
•	Permission Owners: Permissions are granted to three types of owners: the user (file creator), the group (a collection of users), and others (all other system users).
•	10-Character String: Linux represents file permissions with a 10-character string, where the first character indicates file type (e.g., 'd' for directory, '-' for regular file), and subsequent characters denote read, write, and execute permissions for the user, group, and others, respectively.
Checking Permissions
•	ls -l Command: The ls -l command displays detailed information about files and directories, including their permissions, owner, and group.
•	ls -a and ls -la Commands: To view hidden files (those starting with a period), use ls -a. Combining these, ls -la displays permissions for all files and directories, including hidden ones.




Change permissions
Understanding chmod
•	chmod stands for "change mode" and is used to modify permissions on files and directories.
•	The video focuses on the "symbolic" mode for changing permissions, which uses symbols to represent changes.
Symbolic Mode Components
•	Owner Types: u for user, g for group, and o for other are used to specify who the permission change applies to.
•	Mathematical Operators: + is used to add permissions, and - is used to remove permissions.
•	Permission Types: r for read, w for write, and x for execute are used to specify the type of permission being changed.
Applying chmod
•	An example demonstrates changing permissions for access.txt by adding write permissions for the group (g+w) and removing read permissions for others (o-r).
•	The ls -l command can be used before and after chmod to verify the changes in file permissions.

Permission commands
This reading reviews Linux file permissions, how to view and change them, and how to apply the principle of least privilege.
Understanding Linux Permissions
•	Linux permissions are represented by a 10-character string, indicating read (r), write (w), and execute (x) abilities for files and directories.
•	Permissions are assigned to three types of owners: the user (owner of the file), the group (a larger group the owner belongs to), and others (all other system users).
Exploring and Changing Permissions
•	The ls command, especially with options like -l (displays permissions) and -a (displays hidden files), helps investigate existing file and directory permissions.
•	The chmod command is used to change permissions, allowing you to add (+), remove (-), or assign (=) specific permissions for the user (u), group (g), or others (o).
Principle of Least Privilege
•	The principle of least privilege dictates granting only the minimum necessary access to complete a task, reducing security risks.
•	An example demonstrates using chmod g-rw bonuses.txt to remove read and write permissions for a group on a sensitive file, ensuring only the necessary user has access.
•	
ls -a: Displays hidden files. Hidden files start with a period (.) at the beginning.
