Add and delete users
User Authentication and Management
•	Authentication: This is the process of verifying a user's identity to ensure only authorized individuals access the system.
•	Adding and Deleting Users: It is crucial to add new users when they join an organization or group and to delete users when they leave or change groups to maintain system security and access control.
Understanding Root Users and sudo
•	Root User (Superuser): This user has elevated privileges to modify the system, create, modify, or delete any file, and run any program. Only root users or accounts with root privileges can add new users.
•	sudo Command: This command temporarily grants elevated permissions to specific users, offering a more controlled approach than logging in directly as the root user. It stands for "super-user do" and allows users to execute commands with root privileges without switching accounts.
Adding and Deleting Users with Commands
•	useradd Command: This command is used to add a new user to the system. It requires sudo privileges to execute.
•	userdel Command: This command is used to delete a user from the system. Similar to useradd, it also requires sudo privileges.

Responsible use of sudo


This reading focuses on the responsible use of the sudo command and introduces two additional Linux commands, usermod and chown, for managing user authentication and authorization.
Responsible Use of sudo
•	The sudo command allows users to temporarily gain elevated permissions, which is crucial for security analysts to perform administrative tasks without the risks associated with logging in as the root user.
•	It is important to limit sudo access to only those users who genuinely require it and to use it judiciously, as elevated permissions can create security vulnerabilities if compromised.
User Management Commands
